ually T4 is flexible too. But T2 and T5 are terminals of potential sequences? Not terminal. I think T4 is less flexible because it needs T3 or T5/T6, but I have T5, so T4-T5 can form a pair? No, they are different. Actually T4 and T5 are consecutive, so they can form part of a sequence T4-T5-T6 or T3-T4-T5. So T4 is okay.\n\n- Discard W1: I already have a W1 in meld, so extra W1 might be redundant unless I need a pair. W1 is terminal, and there are 3 left unshown, so chance for pair is decent. But if I discard W1, I lose chance for pair with W1. But I have many other tiles for pair. W1 is also useful for pung? Not likely.\n\n- Discard W5, W6, W8, W9: These are part of W sequences. W5 and W6 are adjacent and can form sequence with W4 or W7. W8 and W9 can form sequence with W7. They are valuable for forming chows in the same suit, which could lead to Full Flush or Half Flush if I keep all W tiles. I already have a W Chow, so continuing with W suit might be good. Half Flush requires only one suit and honors. I have J1 honor, and many W tiles. If I discard non-W tiles, I could aim for Half Flush. That's a promising direction: Half Flush is 6 points. Plus other patterns like Pure Double Chow, etc. So I should keep all W tiles and discard non-W tiles.\n\nMy W tiles: hand has W1, W5, W6, W8, W9. Meld has W1,W2,W3. So total W tiles: W1(2), W2(1), W3(1), W5, W6, W8, W9. That's 7 W tiles in hand/meld. I need 14 tiles, so if I aim for Half Flush, I need all tiles to be W or honors. I have J1 honor, so that's okay. I would need to form three more melds from W tiles and maybe a pair from W or honor. That's possible. So I should discard non-W non-honor tiles first.\n\nNon-W non-honor tiles in hand: T2, T4, T5, B5, B7. Among these, which to discard first? B7 is isolated and B8 scarce, so B7 is good discard. B5 is also non-W, but B5 might be useful if I change plan? But for Half Flush, I must discard all non-W non-honor tiles. So discard B7 first.\n\nBut valid actions include Play B7. So that seems a good choice.\n\nHowever, I also have J1 honor, which I should keep for Half Flush because honors are allowed. So not discard J1.\n\nSo discard B7.\n\nBut let's check if there is any immediate risk? Not really.\n\nAlso consider that previous player has B8 pung, so B7 might be safer to discard because B8 is already used, so less chance they need B7 for sequence? But they might need B6 or B9. Not sure.\n\nI think B7 is the best discard to move towards Half Flush.\n\nBut also evaluate other discards: T2, T4, T5 are all non-W, so they should be discarded eventually. But maybe discard B7 first because it's least connected.\n\nMoreover, W9 is terminal, but for Half Flush, terminal is fine as long as it's W suit. So keep W9.\n\nSo final choice: Play B7.\n\nReason: To pursue a Half Flush (6 points) by discarding tiles from other suits, starting with the isolated B7 which has poor sequence potential due to scarce B8.\n\nAnswer: Play B7"}
